---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Remorch payment gateway: deleting a dependency instead of licensing it"
  description="Moving a public crypto payment page to Angular 22 and zoneless meant removing a component library instead of paying to keep it."
  path="/work/remorch-pay"
>
  <a class="back" href="/">Index</a>

  <h1 class="lede">The payment page, rebuilt</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    The page customers pay through. Moving it from Angular 20 to 22 without zone.js meant dealing
    with a component library first.
  </p>

  <dl class="dossier">
    <div>
      <dt>Surface</dt>
      <dd>Public checkout, five routes, crypto payments on Polygon and BSC</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Work</dt>
      <dd>Angular upgrade, zoneless, dependency removal, matching the design</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Stack</dt>
      <dd>Angular 22, signals, native dialog, SCSS</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <section class="shots">
    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="/work/remorch-pay/payment-link.webp"
        alt="A payment link: a dark card showing 24.50 USDC on Polygon, beside pay and connect wallet buttons"
        width="1160"
        height="700"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>A payment link, ready to pay. All data here is mock.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What blocked the upgrade</h2>
    <p>
      This repo was on Angular 20.3 while its sibling was on 22, so shared guidance had to be
      written twice. Dropping zone.js also removes a polyfill from every bundle, along with a whole
      class of "why didn't this repaint" bugs.
    </p>
    <p>
      The component library stood in the way. It expected Angular 17 or 18 and an older zone.js,
      so it couldn't move to 21 or 22, and it depended on the exact package being removed.
    </p>

    <h2>Remove it instead of licensing it</h2>
    <p>
      The library's current major adds a license check. Without a key it draws a banner on every
      page that the app can't style or hide. The free key is per repo, seat capped and expires.
      That has no place over a form where people confirm a transfer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The app used six of its components, and 550 of the 670 lines in its theme file styled
      components it never rendered. Removing them took an afternoon. The currency sheet became a
      native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, and the upgrade went ahead.
    </p>

    <h2>Proof that nothing broke</h2>
    <p>
      Every route was captured before and after. Four of six screens came out byte for byte
      identical. The other two changed on purpose: the currency sheet and the amount card. A later
      pass matched every screen to the design file and checked the layout from 390 to 1440 wide,
      with axe running in the same sweep.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="shots">
    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="/work/remorch-pay/payment-success.webp"
        alt="The payment success screen with a receipt listing receiver, amount, network, rate and transaction hash"
        width="1160"
        height="1000"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>The receipt after a successful payment.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The mock layer</h2>
    <p>
      No real customer appears in these screenshots. Captures run on fixtures: a made-up merchant, a
      domain that can't resolve, a phone number from the reserved range. Eleven scenarios cover
      paths that are hard to hit on demand, like an expired link, a wrong chain or a reverted
      transaction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The mocks are swapped in at build time, not behind a runtime flag. A marker string shows up in
      the mock bundle and not in production, and the production bundle grew by 76 bytes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    <a class="action" href="/work/remorch-panel">The admin panel, same company, opposite decision</a>
  </p>
</Base>
